  "prior_gap": "The previous native same-connection diagnostic retained total_changes=1 after an aborting body update. Except String alone could not return that state; the new API returns an explicit failure state. Its old Except adapter is retained for failure-closing callers.",
  "negative_results": [
    {
      "stage": "Stateful runtime and frame unwinding",
      "cases": 24,
      "failures": 0,
      "reason": "Actual body counts, direct counts, transaction/savepoint data, nested errors and caller rowid restoration match continued native SQL."
    },
    {
      "stage": "RETURNING error expansion",
      "cases": 26,
      "failures": 2,
      "reason": "The actual insertion happened inside the point executor, but user RETURNING then failed before the point state was returned. The already allocated native rowid was lost."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Separate accepted capture and user RETURNING",
      "cases": 27,
      "failures": 0,
      "reason": "Point state is remembered before user outputs evaluate. Both RETURNING errors and an added 1000-frame recursion-failure continuation now match."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Fresh final continuation checkpoint",
      "cases": 27,
      "failures": 0,
      "reason": "Fresh generated inputs pass independently from public file-mode comparisons."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Separate next-work ordinary error diagnostics",
      "cases": 2,
      "failures": 2,
      "reason": "The trigger-free fast path still keeps the preceding changes count on a UNIQUE error and loses the actual insertion rowid on a RETURNING error. These are known failures, not included in the passing continuation metric."
    }
  ],
  "implementation": [
    "A standard pure EStateM carrier retains the actual pending Core state on failure. Successful body statements update it; trigger unwinding restores caller frame registers on success and error. executeStatementEffects returns explicit success or failure state; executeStatement keeps the existing Except adapter for failure-closing callers.",
    "ABORT restores the original outer statement data/context while preserving completed body totalChanges and actual lastInsertRowid. A running statement sets changes to zero; preparation failure preserves the preceding count. Deferred direct counts remain excluded from an aborted row loop. Immediate FK boundary failure excludes its unfinished outer direct count.",
    "Point capture contains only the accepted typed row image. User RETURNING evaluates afterwards, before AFTER programs, against that image and the actual current database/counters. A RETURNING failure therefore retains the actual point rowid without publishing the failed statement data. Actual trigger frames mark schema context; an ordinary RETURNING correlation frame does not by itself become a trigger program."
  ],
  "evaluation_protocols": {
    "public_file_modes": "6990 complete stdout/exit/first-error and independent persisted catalog/rows/integrity comparisons in rollback/WAL modes; both drivers stop at failure.",
    "same_connection": "27 independent memory-connection API comparisons. A test-only transport calls executeStatementEffects, retains every outcome state and runs subsequent complete SQL statements. The pinned sqlite3 shell runs without -bail on the same script. Complete stdout/status and normalized diagnostic lists compare directly. Twelve generated cases use a fresh final seed.",
    "separate_diagnostics": "Two real ordinary-error failures are retained with their driver and outputs; they are the next work. The 88 named probes remain a separate finite survey."
  },
  "proof_scope": "Retained Core/file/physical/counter laws compile. New audited component laws state ABORT restores its original database while retaining the actual runtime total, and output removal preserves INSERT inputs. A new executable group checks post-error SQL counters, AFTER INSERT rowid/frame restoration and RETURNING-before-AFTER failure timing. No global error-state equivalence theorem is claimed.",
  "limitations": [
    "Two separate trigger-free ordinary error diagnostics still fail and are the next fix. Wider error codes/phases, non-trigger point effects, FAIL/IGNORE/ROLLBACK controls, triggered UPSERT, INSTEAD OF/temp triggers, writable CTEs, trigger-aware ALTER and deferred private-function declarations remain work.",
    "Eight of 88 named probes still fail: WITHOUT ROWID, composite primary keys and partial/expression indexes, both modes. These probes do not define global coverage.",
    "UTF-16 writes/setters, native-key/materialization limits, pending new physical allocations and earlier SQL/storage limitations remain. The finite process-kill samples do not establish every syscall/power-loss schedule."
  ],
